# Splunk Enterprise Kubernetes non-secret intake checklist
#
# Copy to template.local for planning. setup.sh does not source this file;
# translate reviewed values into CLI flags. Never place passwords, tokens,
# object-store keys, private-key contents, or license contents here.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared workflow controls
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TARGET=sok                       # sok | pod
# PHASE=render                     # render | preflight | apply | status | all
# OUTPUT_DIR=./splunk-enterprise-k8s-rendered
# ALLOW_UPGRADE=false              # SOK gate; POD records evidence but deploy refuses upgrade
# ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_VERSIONS=false  # reviewed SOK/POD exception; never support evidence
# CONFIRM_SPLUNK_10_4_UPGRADE_READINESS=false
#   # SOK 10.4+ upgrades only: attest restorable platform/KV backups, healthy
#   # KV Store server 7.0+, premium app/add-on compatibility, TLS 1.2+, and
#   # target release-note review. Invalid unless ALLOW_UPGRADE=true.
#
# Existing-bundle phases verify bundle-manifest.json and do not rerender.
# The bundle root must remain current-user-owned mode 0700; tracked files retain
# current-user ownership plus their recorded mode/SHA-256. This detects drift,
# not provenance or a cryptographic signature.
# Use separate render, validate, preflight, apply, and status phases for prod.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splunk Operator for Kubernetes 3.1.0
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ARCHITECTURE=s1                  # s1 | c3 | m4
# OPERATOR_VERSION=3.1.0
# CHART_VERSION=3.1.0              # verified path matches operator version
# SPLUNK_VERSION=10.4.1
# SPLUNK_IMAGE=splunk/splunk:10.4.1
# OPERATOR_IMAGE=docker.io/splunk/splunk-operator:3.1.0
# KUBERNETES_VERSION=1.33          # live preflight also checks the server
# ACCEPT_SPLUNK_GENERAL_TERMS=true # required for every SOK Operator reconcile
#
# Production requires reviewed official local artifacts and digest-pinned
# images. The renderer verifies the exact official 3.1.0 SHA-256 values.
# OPERATOR_CHART_ARCHIVE=/reviewed/splunk-operator-3.1.0.tgz
# ENTERPRISE_CHART_ARCHIVE=/reviewed/splunk-enterprise-3.1.0.tgz
# CRD_MANIFEST=/reviewed/splunk-operator-crds-3.1.0.yaml
# OPERATOR_IMAGE=registry.example.com/splunk/splunk-operator:3.1.0@sha256:<64-hex>
# SPLUNK_IMAGE=registry.example.com/splunk/splunk:10.4.1@sha256:<64-hex>

# Operator RBAC and namespaces:
# OPERATOR_SCOPE=namespace         # namespace | cluster
# NAMESPACE=splunk-operator        # must equal OPERATOR_NAMESPACE in namespace mode
# OPERATOR_NAMESPACE=splunk-operator
# WATCH_NAMESPACES=                # cluster mode only; must include NAMESPACE
# RELEASE_NAME=splunk-enterprise
# OPERATOR_RELEASE_NAME=splunk-operator
#
# Separate namespace example:
# OPERATOR_SCOPE=cluster
# OPERATOR_NAMESPACE=splunk-operator
# NAMESPACE=splunk
# WATCH_NAMESPACES=splunk

# Storage, replicas, and sizing:
# STORAGE_CLASS=gp3
# ETC_STORAGE=10Gi
# VAR_STORAGE=100Gi
# STANDALONE_REPLICAS=1
# INDEXER_REPLICAS=3               # C3 total; M4 per site
# SEARCH_HEAD_REPLICAS=3
# DEPLOYMENT_PROFILE=development   # development | production
#
# Production requires STORAGE_CLASS, SmartStore, license ownership, and
# SmartStore auth. Treat generated resources as a starting point; review a
# splunk-platform-sizing report. Reviewed resource overlays are accepted when
# every Splunk role/deployer keeps complete CPU/memory requests equal to limits.

# M4 failure domains:
# SITE_COUNT=2                      # chart 3.1 M4 preset: exactly 2
# SITE_ZONES=us-west-2a,us-west-2b
# MANAGER_SITE=site1
# SEARCH_HEAD_SITE=site2
# MANAGER_ZONE=us-west-2a
# SEARCH_HEAD_ZONE=us-west-2b
# Three through 63 sites, search-head site0, or custom RF/SF are direct-CR
# handoffs rather than overrides of this fixed two-site chart preset.
# Production status requires replicas within each replicated StatefulSet on
# distinct nodes and confirms each M4 pod's node has the reviewed zone label.

# License ownership. SOK accepts one local license OR an existing manager.
# LICENSE_FILE=/absolute/secure/path/splunk.lic
# EXISTING_LICENSE_MANAGER=lm
# EXISTING_LICENSE_MANAGER_NAMESPACE=splunk  # omit or equal NAMESPACE only
# EXISTING_LICENSE_MANAGER means a ready, same-namespace SOK v4 LicenseManager
# CR. Cross-namespace references, external non-SOK managers, and external
# indexer clusters are direct Helm/CR/secret-management handoffs.

# Monitoring and least-privilege SmartStore AWS IRSA identity:
# DISABLE_MONITORING_CONSOLE=false
# SPLUNK_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=splunk-workload-irsa
# SPLUNK_IRSA_ROLE_ARN=arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/splunk-smartstore
# SPLUNK_IRSA_TOKEN_EXPIRATION=3600  # exact SA annotation; accepted 600..86400

# AWS SmartStore. Do not put access keys here.
# SMARTSTORE_BUCKET=splunk-smartstore-prod
# SMARTSTORE_PREFIX=indexes
# SMARTSTORE_INDEXES=main,security,summary
# SMARTSTORE_PROVIDER=aws           # aws | minio
# SMARTSTORE_REGION=us-west-2
# SMARTSTORE_ENDPOINT=https://s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com
# SMARTSTORE_SECRET_REF=splunk-smartstore-s3
# CONFIRM_SMARTSTORE_INDEX_INVENTORY=true
# CONFIRM_SMARTSTORE_PATH_OWNERSHIP=true  # exclusive active bucket/prefix
#
# For minio, omit SMARTSTORE_REGION and supply an explicit HTTPS endpoint.
#
# Use SMARTSTORE_SECRET_REF for an existing Kubernetes Secret, or
# SPLUNK_SERVICE_ACCOUNT plus SPLUNK_IRSA_ROLE_ARN and AWS_REGION for the
# reviewed AWS IRSA path. The existing ServiceAccount must carry matching role
# and token-expiration annotations plus regional STS=true; the renderer assigns
# it only to Standalone or indexer peers. Production requires one auth path and
# explicit confirmation that SMARTSTORE_INDEXES is complete.
# Upstream calls direct CR installation of SmartStore indexes/configuration a
# temporary method and recommends App Framework. Azure/GCP SmartStore requires
# an App Framework configuration app rather than these provider flags.

# Optional EKS bundle-local kubeconfig helper:
# Authenticate to the approved AWS account/role with duo-sso before preflight.
# EKS_CLUSTER_NAME=splunk-prod      # 1..100: alnum first, then alnum/_/-
# AWS_REGION=us-west-2
#
# Production exact live-target guard (required even when EKS helper is used):
# EXPECTED_KUBE_CONTEXT=arn:aws:eks:us-west-2:123456789012:cluster/splunk-prod
# EXPECTED_API_SERVER=https://EXAMPLE.gr7.us-west-2.eks.amazonaws.com
# EXPECTED_CLUSTER_UID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# SOK 3.1 C3 indexing and ingestion separation (AWS SQS + S3 only):
# INDEXING_INGESTION_SEPARATION=false
# INGESTOR_REPLICAS=3
# QUEUE_PROVIDER=sqs                # sqs | sqs_cp; default sqs
# QUEUE_NAME=splunk-ingest
# QUEUE_DLQ=splunk-ingest-dlq
# QUEUE_REGION=us-west-2            # must match the narrower SOK 3.1 CRD grammar
# QUEUE_ENDPOINT=https://sqs.us-west-2.amazonaws.com
# QUEUE_SECRET_REF=splunk-ingest-queue-credentials
# OBJECT_STORAGE_PATH=splunk-ingest-payloads/messages
# OBJECT_STORAGE_ENDPOINT=https://s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com
#
# Queue, DLQ, bucket, IAM/KMS, endpoint, and Secret creation are external.
# The verified 3.1 path requires QUEUE_SECRET_REF with s3_access_key and
# s3_secret_key and rejects INGESTOR_SERVICE_ACCOUNT/SPLUNK_SERVICE_ACCOUNT.

# Reviewed, non-secret chart overlays. These files are copied into the bundle.
# OPERATOR_VALUES_OVERLAY=/absolute/path/operator-values-overlay.yaml
# ENTERPRISE_VALUES_OVERLAY=/absolute/path/enterprise-values-overlay.yaml
#
# Operator overlays accept only splunkOperator with the exact documented
# metadata/imagePullSecret/scheduling/resources/termination keys. Enterprise
# overlays accept only the seven role roots with labels/annotations, affinity,
# tolerations, topology spread, resources, or validated appRepo. Unknown or
# misspelled keys fail closed. Images, service accounts (including AppRepo
# serviceAccount), storage, schedulerName, services, probes, commands/env,
# security/host settings, arbitrary volumes, extraManifests, defaults/license
# URLs, and namespaces are protected. Never put secrets or Secret objects here.
# Omit appRepo.appInstallPeriodSeconds to keep its upstream 90-second default.
# The strict overlay rejects it; a Splunk Support-directed change is a direct-CR
# handoff. Scale as a separate day-2 operation, not an upgrade;
# pause annotations and administrator-managed PVs require dedicated handoffs.
#
# FIPS uses the standard container images unchanged, but the Kubernetes nodes
# must already be FIPS 140-3-compliant. Ingress remains external: preserve sticky
# Web sessions, at least two forwarder LB IPs, no Indexer Discovery, separate
# clear/encrypted ports, and the selected controller's TLS termination limits.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splunk POD 10.4.0_1.6.0 on Cisco UCS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# POD_PROFILE=pod-small
# POD_PROFILE=pod-medium
# POD_PROFILE=pod-large
# POD_PROFILE=pod-xlarge
# POD_PROFILE=pod-small-es
# POD_PROFILE=pod-medium-es
# POD_PROFILE=pod-large-es
# POD_PROFILE=pod-xlarge-es
# POD_PROFILE=pod-small-itsi
# POD_PROFILE=pod-medium-itsi
# POD_PROFILE=pod-large-itsi
# POD_PROFILE=pod-xlarge-itsi
#
# -es and -itsi are skill selectors. cluster-config.yaml keeps the base profile.

# Coupled bundle and local executable. POD components upgrade together.
# POD_VERSION=10.4.0_1.6.0
# Other coupled versions require ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_VERSIONS plus release and
# compatibility review; that bypass does not make the bundle supported.
# INSTALLER_PATH=/opt/splunk-pod/kubernetes-installer-standalone
# INSTALLER_SHA256=<independently-reviewed-64-hex-sha256>
# CONFIRM_NEW_POD_INSTALL=true     # one-time attestation; required for deploy

# Exact unique node IPs. Every profile has 3 controllers.
# CONTROLLER_IPS=10.10.10.1,10.10.10.2,10.10.10.3
# WORKER_IPS=10.10.10.4,10.10.10.5,...
#
# Worker counts: Small 8, Medium 11, Large 15, X-Large 30.
# ES/ITSI counts: Small 9, Medium 14, Large 18, X-Large 33.

# Bastion access. The private key must be chmod 600 or stricter.
# SSH_USER=splunkadmin
# SSH_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=/absolute/secure/path/pod-ssh-key

# Immutable search-tier identities. Required before a live phase.
# PRIMARY_SEARCH_NAME=core-shc
# SECONDARY_SEARCH_NAME=es-shc       # required for -es or -itsi

# Licenses are comma-separated `.lic` paths. ES and ITSI live runs require two
# physically distinct Enterprise and premium-product licenses.
# LICENSE_FILE=/secure/splunk.lic
# LICENSE_FILE=/secure/splunk.lic,/secure/itsi.lic

# Optional name routing with POD's product certificate (browser warnings are
# possible without customer trust):
# INGRESS_DOMAIN=pod.example.com
#
# Optional trusted name-based TLS requires all four values. The key must be
# owned by the rendering user and mode 0600 or stricter.
# INGRESS_CERTIFICATE_FILE=/secure/fullchain.pem
# INGRESS_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=/secure/privkey.pem
# INGRESS_CA_FILE=/secure/ca-bundle.pem
# INGRESS_DOMAIN=pod.example.com

# POD application scopes. All supplied files must exist before live phases.
# INDEXER_APPS=/apps/Splunk_TA_ForIndexers.tgz  # ES example
# CLUSTER_MANAGER_APPS=/apps/cm-local.tgz
# SEARCH_APPS=/apps/core-shared.tgz
# SEARCH_DEPLOYER_APPS=/apps/deployer-local.tgz
# STANDALONE_APPS=/apps/core-standalone.tgz
# PREMIUM_APPS=/apps/splunk_app_es.spl
# ITSI_APPS=/apps/jdk.tgz,/apps/itsi.tgz,/apps/SA-IndexCreation.tgz,/apps/SA-ITOA.tgz,/apps/SA-UserAccess.tgz,...
# INDEXER_APPS=/apps/SA-IndexCreation.tgz       # ITSI example
# LICENSE_MANAGER_APPS=/apps/SA-ITSI-Licensechecker.tgz,/apps/SA-UserAccess.tgz
# ITSI_SOURCE_BUNDLE=/apps/splunk-it-service-intelligence_4.21.2.spl
# ITSI_SOURCE_SHA256=<reviewed-64-hex-sha256>
# ITSI_JDK_SHA256=<reviewed-64-hex-sha256>
#
# --premium-apps is ES-only. ITSI apps are repackaged and mapped by tier;
# include every ITSI sub-app except SA-ITSI-Licensechecker on the search tier.
# Every -es/-itsi selector retains a primary core tier. Sole-premium and generic
# second-tier layouts require a manual/vendor installer-config handoff.

# Cisco CVD readiness is external: Cisco Intersight profiles, supported UCS and
# Nexus hardware, RHEL 9.6, RAID/ext4 mounts, networking, DNS, and TLS must be
# complete before installer preflight. POD allocates/limits indexer pods at
# 36 CPU/96 GB and search-head pods at 24 CPU/96 GB; prove allocatable capacity
# plus system/failure headroom. SeaweedFS placement/three-way replication and
# profile retention require evidence. X-Large spans two racks/four switches
# and needs full cross-rack reachability.
#
# Small/Medium/Large: 90-day cache + one-year SmartStore retention.
# X-Large: 60-day cache + 180-day SmartStore retention.
# The Manage guide separately says custom indexes receive one-year frozen
# retention; require X-Large effective-setting readback/vendor clarification.
# UF 9.4.1+ requires forcedTimeBasedAutoLB=true for the POD multi-worker path.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Example commands
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Render a namespace-scoped C3 bundle:
# bash skills/splunk-enterprise-kubernetes-setup/scripts/setup.sh \
#   --target sok \
#   --architecture c3 \
#   --kubernetes-version "${KUBERNETES_VERSION}" \
#   --accept-splunk-general-terms
#
# Render a production C3 bundle (all referenced values/files must be reviewed):
# bash skills/splunk-enterprise-kubernetes-setup/scripts/setup.sh \
#   --target sok --architecture c3 --deployment-profile production \
#   --operator-chart-archive "${OPERATOR_CHART_ARCHIVE}" \
#   --enterprise-chart-archive "${ENTERPRISE_CHART_ARCHIVE}" \
#   --crd-manifest "${CRD_MANIFEST}" \
#   --operator-image "${OPERATOR_IMAGE}" --splunk-image "${SPLUNK_IMAGE}" \
#   --expected-kube-context "${EXPECTED_KUBE_CONTEXT}" \
#   --expected-api-server "${EXPECTED_API_SERVER}" \
#   --expected-cluster-uid "${EXPECTED_CLUSTER_UID}" \
#   --storage-class "${STORAGE_CLASS}" \
#   --smartstore-provider "${SMARTSTORE_PROVIDER}" \
#   --smartstore-bucket "${SMARTSTORE_BUCKET}" \
#   --smartstore-region "${SMARTSTORE_REGION}" \
#   --smartstore-indexes "${SMARTSTORE_INDEXES}" \
#   --confirm-smartstore-index-inventory \
#   --confirm-smartstore-path-ownership \
#   --smartstore-secret-ref "${SMARTSTORE_SECRET_REF}" \
#   --license-file "${LICENSE_FILE}" --accept-splunk-general-terms
#
# Render a POD bundle. Render-only can retain placeholders for review:
# bash skills/splunk-enterprise-kubernetes-setup/scripts/setup.sh \
#   --target pod \
#   --pod-profile pod-medium-itsi
#
# Run strict static validation:
# bash skills/splunk-enterprise-kubernetes-setup/scripts/validate.sh \
#   --target pod \
#   --strict
#
# Apply an already reviewed bundle, then run status:
# bash skills/splunk-enterprise-kubernetes-setup/scripts/setup.sh \
#   --target sok --phase apply
# bash skills/splunk-enterprise-kubernetes-setup/scripts/setup.sh \
#   --target sok --phase status
